	"nodes":[
		{"id":"084d7b4d8d6cdaf5","x":-663,"y":411,"width":1083,"height":729,"type":"group","label":"Vena context"},
		{"id":"d180a85244f0cfd7","x":-643,"y":431,"width":1043,"height":689,"type":"text","text":""},
		{"id":"2ca2bfe5f9027eb5","x":-620,"y":460,"width":980,"height":316,"type":"text","text":"### 1. Business Context\n\n- **Organization Details**: Vena is a technology company specializing in financial planning and analysis (FP&A) solutions.\n- **Industry**: Financial Technology (FinTech), specifically focusing on FP&A.\n- **Services/Products**: \n  - AI-powered FP&A platform.\n  - Integration with Microsoft 365 applications.\n  - Tools for budgeting, forecasting, reporting, and collaboration.\n  - Vena Copilot for enhanced analytics and insights.\n- **Goals**: \n  - To streamline financial planning processes.\n  - To enhance collaboration and transparency in financial reporting.\n  - To leverage AI for faster and more accurate financial insights.\n- **Stakeholders**: \n  - Finance teams within organizations.\n  - Business analysts and decision-makers.\n  - IT departments for integration and implementation.\n  - Microsoft as a strategic partner.\n\n### 2. Technical Context\n\n- **Technology Stack**: \n  - AI and machine learning capabilities for enhanced analytics.\n  - Integration with Microsoft 365 (Excel, Power BI, Teams).\n- **Platforms**: \n  - Vena's platform operates primarily as a SaaS (Software as a Service) solution.\n- **Infrastructure**: \n  - Vena CubeFLEX, an OLAP database designed for Excel, serves as the backbone for data management and reporting.\n- **Tools**: \n  - Interactive dashboards for KPI tracking.\n  - Ad-hoc reporting tools integrated within Excel.\n  - Collaboration features including co-authoring and live chat.\n- **Digital Solutions**: \n  - Vena Insights for advanced analytics.\n  - Vena for PowerPoint for data-driven presentations.\n\n### 3. Data & Metrics\n\n- **Performance Metrics**:\n  - 66% faster planning cycles.\n  - 3 weeks saved on the budgeting process.\n  - 95% faster reporting.\n  - 12-day decrease in close times.\n- **User Adoption**: \n  - 87% of companies using other FP&A software still rely on Excel.\n- **Customer Testimonials**: \n  - SaaS companies report 96% faster monthly reporting.\n  - Professional services see 50% faster budget cycles.\n  - Healthcare organizations note improved speed and accuracy in budgeting.\n\n### 4. Other Insights\n\n- **Partnerships**: \n  - Strategic partnership with Microsoft for native integrations and enhanced functionality.\n- **Audience**: \n  - Targeted towards finance teams across various industries including SaaS, healthcare, banking, and professional services.\n- **Community Involvement**: \n  - Engages with organizations to improve financial planning processes and outcomes.\n- **Strategic Notes**: \n  - Emphasis on transforming finance teams from reactive to proactive through advanced technology.\n  - Focus on creating a single source of truth for financial data to enhance decision-making and accountability.\n\nThis structured analysis provides a comprehensive overview of Vena's business and technical contexts, along with relevant data and insights that can inform further KPI development and strategic planning."},
		{"id":"2bd2dab595a02d6d","x":-620,"y":800,"width":980,"height":283,"type":"text","text":"## What is Vena\n\n- Vena Solutions (often just “Vena”) is a software company that provides a cloud-based **Financial Planning & Analysis (FP&A)** platform.\n    \n- The platform is built to work closely with Excel — many of its features integrate with Excel, allowing teams to use familiar tools while adding more structure, collaboration, and automation.\n    \n- Their core purpose is to help organizations plan, budget, forecast, report, and analyze financial data more efficiently — reducing manual efforts, version control issues, and the risk of errors.\n    \n\n---\n\n## Key Features\n\nHere are some of the main capabilities of Vena:\n\n| Feature                                      | Description                                                                                                                                                                                                  |\n| -------------------------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------ |\n| **Excel-native environment**                 | Users can build and maintain models in Excel while using Vena’s backend for data storage, workflow, version control, etc. [Vena Solutions+2Prophix+2](https://www.venasolutions.com/?utm_source=chatgpt.com) |\n| **Budgeting, Forecasting, What-If Modeling** | Flexible modeling, scenario planning, adjusting assumptions, top-down and bottom-up planning. [Vena Solutions+2Prophix+2](https://www.venasolutions.com/?utm_source=chatgpt.com)                             |\n| **Reporting & Dashboards**                   | Real-time reporting, KPI dashboards, variance analysis, drill-downs. [Vena Solutions+2Cube Software+2](https://www.venasolutions.com/solutions/management-reporting?utm_source=chatgpt.com)                  |\n| **Workflow & Collaboration**                 | Central templates, task/approval workflows, collaboration tools to avoid version confusion. [Vena Solutions+1](https://www.venasolutions.com/?utm_source=chatgpt.com)                                        |\n| **Integrations**                             | Integrates with ERPs, CRMs, HR systems etc., plus support for pulling in data from various sources. [Prophix+2Vena Solutions+2](https://www.prophix.com/blog/what-is-vena-solutions/?utm_source=chatgpt.com) |\n| **Add-ons / Extra Modules**                  | Tools such as “Vena Insights” (analytics), “Vena Copilot” (AI agent), etc. [Vena Solutions+1](https://www.venasolutions.com/resources/pricing?utm_source=chatgpt.com)                                        |\n\n---\n\n## Plans & Pricing\n\n- They have different pricing tiers (e.g. _Professional_ and _Complete_). Higher tiers include more advanced features (sandbox environments, premium support, more add-ons). [Vena Solutions+1](https://www.venasolutions.com/resources/pricing?utm_source=chatgpt.com)\n    \n- Pricing is not fully public; you usually have to contact them for a quote. [Prophix+1](https://www.prophix.com/blog/what-is-vena-solutions/?utm_source=chatgpt.com)\n    \n\n---\n\n## Pros\n\nThese are the strengths people often point out:\n\n- Makes use of Excel (familiar tool) but adds needed controls, structure and collaboration. This helps reduce resistance among teams who are used to spreadsheets. [Cube Software+2Vena Solutions+2](https://www.cubesoftware.com/blog/vena-software?utm_source=chatgpt.com)\n    \n- Helps in reducing version control issues (multiple versions of spreadsheets) as many functions are centralized. [Vena Solutions+1](https://www.venasolutions.com/?utm_source=chatgpt.com)\n    \n- Good for “what-if” modeling, scenario planning, aligning different departments on common numbers. [Vena Solutions+1](https://www.venasolutions.com/?utm_source=chatgpt.com)\n    \n\n---\n\n## Cons / Limitations\n\nAnd some of the drawbacks or things people caution about:\n\n- **Performance / scale**: Some users report that when data gets large, certain operations become slow (loading data to cloud, long wait times). [Reddit+1](https://www.reddit.com/r/FPandA/comments/1ch3yve/thoughts_on_vena_solutions/?utm_source=chatgpt.com)\n    \n- **Dependency on setup & service**: Implementation can be expensive and resource-intensive. To get things working well you may need professional services and configuration. [Cube Software+1](https://www.cubesoftware.com/blog/vena-software?utm_source=chatgpt.com)\n    \n- **Learning curve**: Especially trying to set up more complex models, integrating various data sources, and adapting existing Excel workflows. [Cube Software+1](https://www.cubesoftware.com/blog/vena-software?utm_source=chatgpt.com)\n    \n- **Limitations for non-Excel native users**: If teams want to avoid using Excel or want fully-cloud, non-Excel tools, then Vena might feel constrained. Also, for ad hoc analytics, some users prefer other tools. [Cube Software+1](https://www.cubesoftware.com/blog/vena-software?utm_source=chatgpt.com)\n    \n\n---\n\n## Where and for Whom It Works Best\n\n- Mid-market companies that already use Microsoft/Excel heavily and want to retain that interface but improve collaboration, accuracy, and scale. [Prophix+1](https://www.prophix.com/blog/what-is-vena-solutions/?utm_source=chatgpt.com)\n    \n- Companies which have complex financial planning needs, multiple departments, forecasting, budgeting etc., but still want something that people can use without totally leaving behind their Excel-based knowledge.\n    \n- Industries that need regular management reporting, dashboards, and tracking of detailed metrics.\n    \n\n---\n\nIf you like, I can pull up some specific case studies or comparisons of Vena vs alternatives (e.g. Adaptive Insights, Anaplan) so you can see how it stacks up. Do you want me to do that?\n\n##### You said:\n\nExplain me what they does by taking simpler example\n\n###### ChatGPT said:\n\nHere’s a simpler example to help show _what Vena does_ in practice — think of a small company, and how Vena helps with its budgeting, forecasting, analysis, etc.\n\n---\n\n## Example: “BrightCups” — a small chain of tea cafes\n\nLet’s say **BrightCups** has 5 cafes, one central finance team, and they want to plan for the next year: figuring out budget, predicting sales, and seeing what happens if things change. Previously, they used lots of Excel files that got emailed around, lots of versions, manual copying, delays, errors. They want a better way.\n\nHere’s how Vena might help, step by step.\n\n---\n\n## Step 1: Collecting past data & setting up\n\n- BrightCups has sales, expense, overhead numbers from previous years in their POS system, payroll, supplier invoices, etc. These are in different places.\n    \n- With Vena, they connect those data sources (ERP, point-of-sale, HR/payroll etc.) so actual numbers flow into one central database. That means the finance team doesn’t have to manually copy numbers from different systems into Excel. [Vena Solutions+2TrustRadius+2](https://www.venasolutions.com/solutions/financial-planning-analysis?utm_source=chatgpt.com)\n    \n\n---\n\n## Step 2: Budgeting / Planning\n\n- BrightCups wants to budget for next year. They decide budget items: e.g. rent, salary costs, cost of tea leaves & milk, utilities, marketing, maintenance etc.\n    \n- Each café manager gets a budgeting template. Since Vena uses an Excel-like interface, café managers can fill in their expected sales, cost of goods (milk, tea, sugar), staff hours etc., using templates that are familiar. But instead of sending back multiple versions, they submit those templates through Vena. The data is stored in the central system. [Vena Solutions+3Vena Solutions+3TrustRadius+3](https://www.venasolutions.com/solutions/financial-planning-analysis?utm_source=chatgpt.com)\n    \n- The finance team can define whether the budget is “top-down” (they set some targets and cafés align) or “bottom-up” (each café proposes their numbers) or even driver-based (e.g. sales * cost of goods margin, it's not fixed). Vena supports zero-based, driver-based etc. [Vena Solutions+1](https://www.venasolutions.com/solutions/financial-planning-analysis?utm_source=chatgpt.com)\n    \n\n---\n\n## Step 3: Forecasting & What-if Scenarios\n\n- Halfway through the year, BrightCups notices tea leaf prices are going up or maybe a new competitor opened nearby, so sales might drop. They want to see how that will affect profit.\n    \n- With Vena, finance can take the actual data so far + adjust assumptions (e.g. tea cost increases by 20%, sales drop by 10%) and run a _what-if scenario_. Because Vena has integrated templates and data, the forecast rolls forward automatically and shows how profit, cash flow, etc. gets affected. [Vena Solutions+2Vena Solutions+2](https://www.venasolutions.com/blog/finance-led-planning-blog?utm_source=chatgpt.com)\n    \n- They might also run multiple scenarios: _best case_ (tea cost stable, sales grow), _expected case_, _worst case_ (cost rises, sales drop) and compare. [Vena Solutions](https://www.venasolutions.com/blog/how-to-use-scenario-planning-for-decision-making?utm_source=chatgpt.com)\n    \n\n---\n\n## Step 4: Reporting, comparing budget vs actuals\n\n- Each month, BrightCups gets “actuals” (how much sales actually happened, how much was spent). Vena automatically pulls those in from connected systems. [Vena Solutions+2TrustRadius+2](https://www.venasolutions.com/solutions/financial-planning-analysis?utm_source=chatgpt.com)\n    \n- Finance can now create reports comparing actual vs budget, see variances (where costs are higher than expected, or sales lower). For example: \"This café’s milk costs are 20% over budget because supplier price increased\" or \"Marketing spend is under budget because we delayed some campaigns\". [Vena Solutions+1](https://www.venasolutions.com/solutions/financial-planning-analysis?utm_source=chatgpt.com)\n    \n- The reports can be shared, dashboards made, so café managers or executives can see performance with up-to-date numbers.\n    \n\n---\n\n## Step 5: Workflow, collaboration & version control\n\n- Because Vena has workflow features, the budget/forecast tasks can be assigned: e.g. café managers need to submit by a date; finance reviews, then leadership approves. Everyone knows the status: who has submitted, what’s overdue. This replaces “someone sending reminders by email”. [JMT Consulting+1](https://jmtconsulting.com/resources/video/jmt-vena-the-perfect-budgeting-planning-combination/?utm_source=chatgpt.com)\n    \n- Version control / audit trails: if someone changes a number, you can see who changed it, when, what it was before. If you want to compare last year’s forecast with this year’s forecast etc. [Vena Solutions+1](https://www.venasolutions.com/solutions/financial-planning-analysis?utm_source=chatgpt.com)\n    \n\n---\n\n## Bottom-line Benefit in this example\n\n- Much less time wasted emailing spreadsheets around, fixing mistakes from manual copying, tracking down the latest version.\n    \n- Faster budget cycles: maybe instead of 4-5 weeks, they do it in 2-3 weeks.\n    \n- Better decision-making: if cost of goods rises, they can see impact immediately, try scenarios.\n    \n- More transparency: managers know where their cafés stand vs plan; leadership sees whole company snapshot.\n    \n\n---\n\nIf you like, I can build a **toy model** (with numbers) to show how this would look in BrightCups using Vena vs doing it purely with Excel. Do you want that?\n\n##### You said:\n\nso basically they build a ETL pipeline right?? but only related to the finance of a org right?\n\n###### ChatGPT said:\n\nYes — in many ways what you said is correct: Vena does a lot like what an ETL pipeline does, but it’s more than just ETL. I’ll explain what Vena _does_ vs what “just ETL” means, to help you see the difference.\n\n---\n\n## What is ETL\n\nETL stands for **Extract, Transform, Load**:\n\n1. **Extract** = pulling data from source systems (ERP, CRM, HR systems, etc.)\n    \n2. **Transform** = cleaning, standardizing, combining, doing calculations, making data usable\n    \n3. **Load** = putting that cleaned, transformed data into a target system / database so it can be used\n    \n\nETL pipelines are about moving data, preparing it, and making it ready for analysis or use downstream.\n\n---\n\n## What Vena does — is it just ETL?\n\nYes, Vena includes ETL-type capabilities (among others). But Vena also builds layers on top of that so users in finance can do budgeting, forecasting, reporting, scenario-analysis, workflows, etc. So it’s not just moving & cleaning data; Vena uses that data in specific, finance-focused ways. Here’s how Vena overlaps with ETL, and where it adds extra value.\n\n---\n\n## How Vena overlaps with ETL\n\nHere are some parts of Vena that are very ETL-like:\n\n- **Data integration**: Vena integrates with many source systems (ERP, accounting, HRIS, CRM, etc.). It can pull data from all these systems via APIs, native connectors, flat files, etc. [Vena Solutions+2Vena Solutions+2](https://www.venasolutions.com/product/integrations?utm_source=chatgpt.com)\n    \n- **ETL templates / tools**: Vena provides ETL templates to move data (files) into Vena tables etc. There's an ETL tool in Vena. [developers.venasolutions.com+2GitHub+2](https://developers.venasolutions.com/docs/setting-up-a-vena-template?utm_source=chatgpt.com)\n    \n- **Scheduling ETL & monitoring**: It supports automated ETL job scheduling and has notifications for failures etc. [Vena Solutions](https://www.venasolutions.com/newsroom/vena-announces-new-integrated-planning-functionality?utm_source=chatgpt.com)\n    \n\nSo yes, it _does_ the extract/transform/load, at least for finance‐relevant data.\n\n---\n\n## Extra stuff Vena does beyond ETL\n\nHere are things Vena does _in addition_ to what a typical ETL tool/pipeline would do:\n\n1. **Finance modeling & scenario planning**  \n    Once data is inside Vena, finance teams can build forecasts, budgets, run “what-if” scenarios (e.g. “if sales drop 10% / costs go up”). ETL tools don’t usually offer that. [Vena Solutions+1](https://www.venasolutions.com/blog/vena-vs-anaplan?utm_source=chatgpt.com)\n    \n2. **Budgeting, planning & forecasting workflows**  \n    Assigning responsibility (which department enters what), approvals, reviews, etc. People collaborate. ETL doesn’t handle that. [Vena Solutions+1](https://www.venasolutions.com/blog/vena-vs-anaplan?utm_source=chatgpt.com)\n    \n3. **Reporting and dashboards**  \n    Generating reports, dashboards, comparisons of budget vs actual vs forecast, visualizations. ETL tools just prepare data; Vena helps use it. [Vena Solutions+2Vena Solutions+2](https://www.venasolutions.com/product/integrations?utm_source=chatgpt.com)\n    \n4. **Excel integration / user-friendly interface**  \n    Many finance users prefer Excel. Vena lets them work in Excel, or Excel-like templates, while keeping behind it a central database for consistency. This reduces errors, version mismatch etc. ETL tools usually don’t have this user interface & reporting dimension. [Vena Solutions+1](https://www.venasolutions.com/blog/vena-vs-anaplan?utm_source=chatgpt.com)\n    \n5. **Auditing, version control, collaboration & governance**  \n    Tracking who changed what, when; ensuring data integrity; ensuring a “single source of truth” etc. Finance data has compliance, governance issues. Vena helps with that. ETL alone usually doesn’t cover these UX / governance features. [Apply to Supply+2Vena Solutions+2](https://www.applytosupply.digitalmarketplace.service.gov.uk/g-cloud/services/475766160006201?utm_source=chatgpt.com)\n    \n\n---\n\n## So, summary\n\n- Yes, you can think of Vena as having an ETL pipeline _part_ — it extracts finance & operations related data, transforms it, loads into its own system.\n    \n- But Vena is more: it is an entire **FP&A (Financial Planning & Analysis) platform** built on top of those data flows. It includes modeling, reporting, collaboration, dashboards, scenario analysis etc.\n    "}
	],
